+2.  **Gaussian Normal Field:**
+    The normal field $\mathcal{N}(x)$ is the normalized version of $V(x)$ [S1]:
+    $$ \mathcal{N}(x) = \begin{cases} \frac{V(x)}{\|V(x)\|} & \text{if } \|V(x)\| > 0 \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} $$
 
-**Formulas and Definitions:**
-*   $v(x)$: Vacancy.
-*   $\nabla \log v(x)$: Gradient of the log-vacancy.
-*   $G_i(x)$: The $i$-th Gaussian function.
-*   $\nabla \log(1 - G_i(x))$: The gradient of the log-vacancy contribution of the $i$-th Gaussian.
-*   $n_i^T(x-\mu_i) \geq 0$: The condition that point $x$ lies in the half-space defined by normal $n_i$ at mean $\mu_i$. This acts as a mask to ensure only the "front" or oriented side of the Gaussian contributes to the gradient sum [S1].
+**Derivation and Disambiguation:**
+The derivation relies on the assumption that locally, each term in the sum has the same sign as the total gradient or is zero [S1]. Specifically, under the wrapping assumption, $\nabla \log(1 - G_i(x))$ points from occupied space toward empty space when $n_i^T(x-\mu_i) \geq 0$ [S1]. This allows the removal of absolute values from the general identity:
+$$ |\mathbf{w} \cdot \nabla \log v(\mathbf{x})| = \sum_i \mathbb{1}_{n_i^T(\mathbf{x}-\mu_i) \geq 0} |\mathbf{w} \cdot \nabla \log(1 - G_i(\mathbf{x}))| $$
+to the vector equation:
+$$ \mathbf{w} \cdot \nabla \log v(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_i \mathbf{w} \cdot \mathbb{1}_{n_i^T(\mathbf{x}-\mu_i) \geq 0} \nabla \log(1 - G_i(\mathbf{x})) $$
+Since this holds for arbitrary direction $\mathbf{w} \in S^2$, the vector form (Eq. 34) follows [S1].
 
